;;; `std.closure/infer-closure` — functional ∧ total ⟹ `(__closed__ R)`, and
;;; the soundness caveat **exhibited** rather than described.
;;;
;;; ── What the rule promises ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;;   (functional R) ∧ (total R)  ⟹  (__closed__ R)
;;;
;;; `(__closed__ R)` tells the hypothesis generator never to speculate an
;;; R-fact: R's extension is taken as fixed. The witness is **operational** —
;;; a total function has exactly one image per domain element, so *given enough
;;; elimination* every R-fact arrives by saturation rather than by guessing.
;;;
;;; ── The caveat, which is larger than the promise ───────────────────────────
;;;
;;; "Given enough elimination" is the whole of it. If R needs hypothesis
;;; **branching** to be solved — as every bijective attribute relation of the
;;; Zebra puzzle does — closing it prunes the branches the search depends on.
;;; The module's header is a boxed warning saying so, and the import is the
;;; only gate there is.
;;;
;;; The census's §6 named this the one place the corpus could document a
;;; soundness caveat **by exhibiting it**. This file is that, and the exhibit
;;; is a number:
;;;
;;;   with    (import std.closure …)   Solution,  k = 1    ← what this file claims
;;;   without it                       Ambiguity, k = 54
;;;
;;; Three agents, three colours, one given. `functional-negative` excludes
;;; Green and Blue for Ann and `injective-negative` excludes Red for Bob and
;;; Cid, and there it stops: Bob and Cid have two colours each, so the relation
;;; is **not** determined by saturation and a guess is what would settle it.
;;; Closed, the generator never makes that guess, and the search reports the
;;; root as the unique model — with two of the three agents nowhere at all.
;;;
;;; Nothing is *wrong* in that answer. It is a completeness hazard rather than
;;; a soundness one: every fact in the model is true, and fifty-three models
;;; that were equally true have silently stopped existing. That is exactly the
;;; failure the boxed warning describes, and it is the reason the marker is
;;; opt-in by import and must never be imported into the zebra family.
;;;
;;; (Fifty-three, and not one — the unclosed count is 54 rather than the 2 a
;;; reader expects, because a state that places one agent and leaves two
;;; unplaced is **complete** in an open world: nothing obliges an agent to wear
;;; anything, so every partial placement counts. Turning `total` into a
;;; first-class obligation is M1d's subject
;;; (docs/history/m1d_satisfiability/), and until it arrives the two
;;; numbers measure slightly different things. The comparison survives that:
;;; 54 → 1 is not a rounding.)
;;;
;;; ── What the expectation means ─────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `wears` is deliberately **absent** from `:no-hypothesis`: the generator is
;;; free to speculate about it, and what stops it is the derived
;;; `(__closed__ wears)`. Closing `wears` at the one given edge is therefore
;;; the claim that no speculation survived — and, read the other way, the
;;; record of the model that was lost.

(import std.macro     :symbols (forall))
(import std.bijection :symbols (functional-negative injective-negative
                                domain-elimination))
(import std.closure   :symbols (infer-closure))

(relation is-a  T T          :why "{?1} is a {?2}")
(relation wears Agent Colour :why "{?1} wears {?2}")

(is-a Ann Agent)  (is-a Bob Agent)   (is-a Cid Agent)
(is-a Red Colour) (is-a Green Colour) (is-a Blue Colour)

;; The two markers are **authored**, not derived from `(bijective wears)`, and
;; that is what makes the ⊥— sorry, the *closure* — attributable: a version of
;; `infer-closure` that needed only one of the two would still fire if
;; `bijective-properties` had put all four cardinality markers here. It has
;; not: `injective` and `surjective` are absent from this program, so a rule
;; that read either of them derives nothing and the search speculates.
(functional wears)
(total      wears)

;; The operational activators, declared directly — the module's documented
;; route for a relation that is a function but not a full bijection.
(functional-negative wears is-a)
(injective-negative  wears is-a)
(domain-elimination  wears is-a)

(wears Ann Red)

(query
  :goal (wears ?who ?colour)
  ;; `wears` is deliberately not here — see above.
  :no-hypothesis (is-a)
  :expect (model (wears Ann Red)
                 (not (wears Ann Green)) (not (wears Ann Blue))
                 (not (wears Bob Red))   (not (wears Cid Red))))
